#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502401 :question/problem "![](images/92/92fcd6bef2b85d707fbe936c7aaa4870b64c158ca12f614123c0957dfa2a0efe.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/39/39cc72f1b5da145c6c475c0759ab8df4bead1a136edadfae7c1e17291db451b1.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=1.5$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2+(2)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{5}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502408 :question/problem "![](images/73/733f3c7b5fde90bb1e33c1988cfc45136c1be0be83464e603ab796128bc6d74c.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/a0/a00c60819288df1d7331b0dcb0f92641378563ef916c3c934e9f11ccade084da.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=2$ and $BH=5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2)^2+(5)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4+25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{29}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $\\sqrt{29}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{29}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502413 :question/problem "![](images/b5/b58a1e76ec7f661d263be05a4e17826c99b1bce49e68ec5965c9ae91c4222e3c.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/c6/c6d8bb8e64840dc44a7cd8e8fcab4c415147038f43eb12ad5186de620d8499b3.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=4$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4)^2+(2)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16+4} \\\\\n&= 2 \\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $2 \\sqrt{5}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502417 :question/problem "![](images/27/27a852815532c69325ee58633141af9b34215fdb4e53058c8fa0fc3f73e74d37.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f1/f1880e8d87ec11f1199b3283f5eb4dfa32d4794bfc11e40f9239469d98ac5fe3.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=5$ and $BH=3$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(5)^2+(3)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25+9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{34}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $\\sqrt{34}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{34}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502428 :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2a2003ffdc18b5cc19abbe61346906d9ddbe45021109b41e88818d40dd05ee3.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/23/23a7a8c3471a8f297b1daf5bf52f4314e2360b215083bcb379605801a5984890.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=4$ and $BH=1$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4)^2+(1)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16+1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $\\sqrt{17}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502430 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $6$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $8$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=6^2+8^2\\\\\n&=36+64\\\\\n&=100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{100}=10\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{10}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502436 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid a|= 10$, while the vector $b$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid b|= 15$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=10^2+15^2\\\\\n&=100+225\\\\\n&=325\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{325}=5 \\sqrt{13}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{5 \\sqrt{13}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502437 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid a|= 10$, while the vector $b$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid b|= 14$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=10^2+14^2\\\\\n&=100+196\\\\\n&=296\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{296}=2 \\sqrt{74}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{74}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502451 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $8$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $15$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=8^2+15^2\\\\\n&=64+225\\\\\n&=289\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{289}=17\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{17}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502459 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid a|= 10$, while the vector $b$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid b|= 13$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=10^2+13^2\\\\\n&=100+169\\\\\n&=269\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{269}=\\sqrt{269}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{269}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502475 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $7$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $24$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=7^2+24^2\\\\\n&=49+576\\\\\n&=625\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{625}=25\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{25}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502480 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $5$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $12$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=5^2+12^2\\\\\n&=25+144\\\\\n&=169\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{169}=13\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{13}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502481 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $2$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $5$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=2^2+5^2\\\\\n&=4+25\\\\\n&=29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{29}=\\sqrt{29}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502485 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $2$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $6$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=2^2+6^2\\\\\n&=4+36\\\\\n&=40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{40}=2 \\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{10}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502487 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $2$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $7$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=2^2+7^2\\\\\n&=4+49\\\\\n&=53\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{53}=\\sqrt{53}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{53}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502493 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $9$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $12$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=9^2+12^2\\\\\n&=81+144\\\\\n&=225\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{225}=15\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{15}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502512 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $2$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $4$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=2^2+4^2\\\\\n&=4+16\\\\\n&=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{20}=2 \\sqrt{5}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{5}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502513 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $2$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $3$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=2^2+3^2\\\\\n&=4+9\\\\\n&=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{13}=\\sqrt{13}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502526 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid a|= 10$, while the vector $b$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid b|= 11$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=10^2+11^2\\\\\n&=100+121\\\\\n&=221\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{221}=\\sqrt{221}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{221}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502529 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid a|= 10$, while the vector $b$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid b|= 12$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=10^2+12^2\\\\\n&=100+144\\\\\n&=244\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{244}=2 \\sqrt{61}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{61}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502537 :question/problem "The inequality $- 3\\sqrt{5} < u \\le - 3\\sqrt{3}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality\n\n$$\n- 3\\sqrt{5} < u \\le - 3\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $- 3\\sqrt{5}$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $( $.\n- The right endpoint $- 3\\sqrt{3}$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $]$.\n\nTherefore, the interval that corresponds to the given inequality is\n\n$$\nu ∈ (- 3\\sqrt{5}, - 3\\sqrt{3}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502542 :question/problem "The interval $u ∈ [- \\frac{3}{4}, \\frac{4}{3})$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\nu ∈ [- \\frac{3}{4}, \\frac{4}{3})\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $- \\frac{3}{4}$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $\\le$.\n- The right endpoint $\\frac{4}{3}$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $<$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n- \\frac{3}{4} \\le u < \\frac{4}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502553 :question/problem "The interval $y ∈ (- 2\\sqrt{3}, 2\\sqrt{2}]$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\ny ∈ (- 2\\sqrt{3}, 2\\sqrt{2}]\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $- 2\\sqrt{3}$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $<$.\n- The right endpoint $2\\sqrt{2}$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $\\le$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n- 2\\sqrt{3} < y \\le 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502562 :question/problem "The inequality $-8 < y \\le 2\\sqrt{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality\n\n$$\n-8 < y \\le 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-8$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $( $.\n- The right endpoint $2\\sqrt{2}$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $]$.\n\nTherefore, the interval that corresponds to the given inequality is\n\n$$\ny ∈ (-8, 2\\sqrt{2}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502563 :question/problem "The interval $y ∈ [-3, 1)$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\ny ∈ [-3, 1)\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-3$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $\\le$.\n- The right endpoint $1$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $<$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n-3 \\le y < 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502567 :question/problem "The inequality $- \\frac{11}{2} \\le u < - \\frac{1}{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality\n\n$$\n- \\frac{11}{2} \\le u < - \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $- \\frac{11}{2}$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $[$.\n- The right endpoint $- \\frac{1}{2}$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $) $.\n\nTherefore, the interval that corresponds to the given inequality is\n\n$$\nu ∈ [- \\frac{11}{2}, - \\frac{1}{2})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502569 :question/problem "The interval $y ∈ (-\\sqrt{5}, -\\sqrt{3}]$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\ny ∈ (-\\sqrt{5}, -\\sqrt{3}]\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-\\sqrt{5}$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $<$.\n- The right endpoint $-\\sqrt{3}$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $\\le$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{5} < y \\le -\\sqrt{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502574 :question/problem "The interval $y ∈ (- \\frac{5}{3}, - \\frac{1}{3}]$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\ny ∈ (- \\frac{5}{3}, - \\frac{1}{3}]\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $- \\frac{5}{3}$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $<$.\n- The right endpoint $- \\frac{1}{3}$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $\\le$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n- \\frac{5}{3} < y \\le - \\frac{1}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502580 :question/problem "The inequality $- \\frac{11}{2} \\le t < \\frac{1}{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality\n\n$$\n- \\frac{11}{2} \\le t < \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $- \\frac{11}{2}$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $[$.\n- The right endpoint $\\frac{1}{2}$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $) $.\n\nTherefore, the interval that corresponds to the given inequality is\n\n$$\nt ∈ [- \\frac{11}{2}, \\frac{1}{2})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502592 :question/problem "The inequality $-13 \\le t < 4$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality\n\n$$\n-13 \\le t < 4\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-13$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $[$.\n- The right endpoint $4$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $) $.\n\nTherefore, the interval that corresponds to the given inequality is\n\n$$\nt ∈ [-13, 4)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502593 :question/problem "The interval $u ∈ [1, 7)$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\nu ∈ [1, 7)\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $\\le$.\n- The right endpoint $7$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $<$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n1 \\le u < 7\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502615 :question/problem "The inequality $- 3\\sqrt{5} \\le t < - 2\\sqrt{6}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality\n\n$$\n- 3\\sqrt{5} \\le t < - 2\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $- 3\\sqrt{5}$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $[$.\n- The right endpoint $- 2\\sqrt{6}$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $) $.\n\nTherefore, the interval that corresponds to the given inequality is\n\n$$\nt ∈ [- 3\\sqrt{5}, - 2\\sqrt{6})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502620 :question/problem "The inequality $-13 \\le u < 3$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality\n\n$$\n-13 \\le u < 3\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-13$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $[$.\n- The right endpoint $3$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $) $.\n\nTherefore, the interval that corresponds to the given inequality is\n\n$$\nu ∈ [-13, 3)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502629 :question/problem "The inequality $1 \\le x \\le 9$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $9$ *are* included.\n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,9{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,9]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502634 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x ∈ [-  3 \\sqrt{5}, - 2 \\sqrt{2}]$?" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only brackets. So, the endpoints are *included*.\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}- 3 \\sqrt{5}, - 2 \\sqrt{2}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n- 3 \\sqrt{5} \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\le}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\le}}\\,\\, - 2 \\sqrt{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502645 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x ∈ [-  2 \\sqrt{3}, 2 \\sqrt{2}]$?" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only brackets. So, the endpoints are *included*.\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}- 2 \\sqrt{3}, 2 \\sqrt{2}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n- 2 \\sqrt{3} \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\le}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\le}}\\,\\, 2 \\sqrt{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502663 :question/problem "The inequality $-11\\le x\\le 3$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-11$ and $3$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n-11\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-11,3]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-11,3].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502670 :question/problem "The inequality $- \\sqrt{3} \\le x \\le \\frac{3}{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $- \\sqrt{3}$ and $\\frac{3}{2}$ *are* included.\n\n$$\n- \\sqrt{3}\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, \\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}- \\sqrt{3},\\frac{3}{2}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [- \\sqrt{3},\\frac{3}{2}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502690 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x ∈ [-  2 \\sqrt{5}, - \\frac{4}{3}]$?" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only brackets. So, the endpoints are *included*.\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}- 2 \\sqrt{5}, - \\frac{4}{3}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n- 2 \\sqrt{5} \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\le}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\le}}\\,\\, - \\frac{4}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502691 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x\\in [-4,-2]$?" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $(,)$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only brackets. So, the endpoints *are* included. \n\n$$\nx\\in [-4,-2]\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-4\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,-2\n$$"}]}
